se it contains four clubs. Any single club in the opponent\'s hand completes a Flush, which beats your pair of Jacks. Even without a flush, a Queen beats your Jacks. Your opponent bet the flop but checked the turn; the check on the turn suggests they might not have a strong made hand or were controlling the pot size, but the arrival of the 4th club on the river makes the board extremely wet. Betting here creates a "value trap"—worse hands (like a lower pair or Ace high without a club) will fold, while better hands (any Flush, Trip Queens, Two Pair) will call or raise. Checking is the best option to try and realize your showdown value for free or induce a bluff you can evaluate later, though usually, you would fold to a significant river bet on this texture.\n\nReason: With four clubs on the board, a pair of Jacks is a weak hand. Betting offers no value because worse hands fold and better hands call. Checking is the optimal defensive play to potentially see a cheap showdown.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
