rful hand. The board contains both flush and straight draws (two spades, two diamonds, connected cards). Your opponent checked the flop, indicating they likely do not have a strong Queen or are playing passively. Checking again risks giving a free card to a flush or straight draw. Betting extracts value from weaker hands like top pair (Queens) or pocket pairs, and charges opponents to see the river if they are drawing.\n\nReason: You have a disguised monster hand after checking the flop. To maximize winnings and protect against the various draws on the board, you should bet. A moderate bet builds the pot without immediately scaring off weaker made hands.\n\nAnswer: 300'}
